harness.lua — load EllesmereUISecretsDiag outside the game and drive it

    cd tools/diag && lua5.1 harness.lua

Stubs enough of the WoW API to load all five files in .toc order, fire
ADDON_LOADED, and dispatch every command. It catches what a syntax check
cannot: load-order faults, a helper that moved between files, nil calls during
registration, and dispatch bugs. It also drives the live CPU monitor against a
profiler stub whose readings change over time, and asserts that:

  * the table repaints at all
  * Average mode reports a different number from live, and relabels its column
    (an average that silently echoes the live value looks perfectly fine)
  * the graph draws line segments, grows the window, and shows its panel
  * All pins every listed module and None returns to auto without emptying
    the plot
  * the recorder does NOT walk every addon's memory once per sample -- that
    call is the expensive part of sampling at a dungeon-rate interval, so it
    runs on its own slower cycle (proven by counting the stubbed calls)

Two pieces of stub fidelity that took a bug each to learn, and that anything
added here has to preserve:

  Show and Hide fire OnShow/OnHide, as they do in the client. Without that,
  anything a frame does on show is never exercised and the window appears to
  work while doing nothing.

  The catch-all __index fabricates methods for missing keys, but returns nil
  for underscore-prefixed ones. Those are the stub's own data fields, and
  handing back a function makes every unset field read as truthy — which shows
  up as assertions passing on garbage rather than as an error.

It writes harness-sv.lua, a SavedVariables file in the client's own format,
which is what feeds ../perf/euidiag-perf.py in the round trip below.

    lua5.1 harness.lua && ../perf/euidiag-perf.py harness-sv.lua

Exit status is 0 only if every dispatch ran. Command errors are caught and
reported by the tool itself rather than raised, so also check the output for
unexpected [ERR] lines:

    lua5.1 harness.lua | grep 'ERR\]'

Three of those are deliberate (a bad `eval` expression, and export/drop with no
recording present); anything else is a regression.

This is a smoke test, not a substitute for playing. It proves the plumbing
holds together; it says nothing about whether a probe reports the truth, since
every API it calls is a stub returning a constant. Anything that depends on
real secret values, real taint, or real CPU attribution has to be confirmed in
game.
